Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?arm64:=20dts:=20exynos:=20google:=20gs101-raven?=
 =?UTF-8?q?:=20CPU=20and=20GPU=20critical=20trips=20at=20115=20=C2=B0C?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The CPU zones' critical trips at 110 °C powered raven off in the middle
of a package install: big-thermal read 110 °C once. These sensors
report the cluster hotspot through ACPM and can jump by 30 °C for a
single sample under a short burst of load (logged once a second: big
58 -> 89 -> 60 °C, mid and little up 11-14 °C, in the second the lock
screen came up). With polling at 100-250 ms the passive trips can't
react to such a burst, and the critical trip then shuts the phone down
for a transient.

Google's raven (gs101.dtsi in its GPL kernel) throttles the CPUs from
90 °C and only has a "hot" trip at 115 °C, no critical one. Keep the
throttling from 75 °C and move raven's CPU critical trips to 115 °C, so
a sustained overheat still powers off but a one-sample spike doesn't.
The GPU zone has no cooling yet, and a panfrost burst may spike its
sensor the same way, so it gets the same 115 °C. gs101.dtsi keeps
110 °C for other boards; the ISP and TPU zones are unchanged.

This trip is not below the TMU's own hardware trip; it is the same
temperature. Read on raven through the firmware (TMU_IPC_REG_READ),
both TMUs have the thermal trip enabled in CONTROL, and on every probe
with interrupts enabled the bootloader set the eight rising thresholds
to 80, 85 ... 110, 115 °C, decoded with each probe's TRIMINFO (codes at
25 and 85 °C, about 194 and 305) and checked against ACPM's own reading
of the live codes (within 1 °C on all six sensors). The probes with no
interrupts keep a top threshold near 122 °C. So a sustained overheat may
end in the hardware reset rather than this orderly shutdown; going below
115 °C brings back the spike power-offs above, so the trip stays a
backstop.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: saiththerobo <the.tom.sharp@gmail.com>
---
 .../boot/dts/exynos/google/gs101-raven.dts    | 26 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/gs101-raven.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/gs101-raven.dts
index 991d62731..5bfe8d9fb 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/gs101-raven.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/gs101-raven.dts
@@ -233,3 +233,29 @@ ts-spi-sel-hog {
 	regulator-min-microvolt = <3300000>;
 	regulator-max-microvolt = <3300000>;
 };
+
+/*
+ * The CPU zones report the cluster hotspot through ACPM, which can jump by
+ * 30 °C for one sample under a short burst of load. Google's raven
+ * throttles from 90 °C and only has a "hot" trip at 115 °C: keep our
+ * throttling from 75 °C and power off at a sustained 115 °C. The GPU zone
+ * gets the same until it has GPU cooling: a panfrost burst may spike it
+ * the same way. The bootloader sets the TMU's own hardware trip at 115 °C
+ * too, so this trip is a backstop: a sustained overheat may end in that
+ * hardware reset rather than an orderly shutdown.
+ */
+&big_crit {
+	temperature = <115000>;
+};
+
+&mid_crit {
+	temperature = <115000>;
+};
+
+&little_crit {
+	temperature = <115000>;
+};
+
+&gpu_crit {
+	temperature = <115000>;
+};
